course: religion-ethics
lesson: 42
title: Finders keepers and what belongs to whom
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Children in the playground often shout "finders keepers, losers weepers", but a lost thing does not stop belonging to its owner just because you found it.
explain: Picture finding a wallet with forty pounds and an address card inside: somewhere a person is turning out their pockets in a panic, and you are the one who can end that worry.
explain: The commandment "You shall not steal" (Exodus 20:15) covers more than grabbing things: keeping extra change, borrowing without asking, copying someone's work and using what was stolen are all ways of taking what is not yours.
explain: Christians believe everything is a gift from God and we are caretakers of it, so we treat other people's things as carefully as we would want ours treated, which is the Golden Rule.
explain: Some cases are hard and people disagree, such as a starving person taking bread; a good answer says taking is wrong but also asks why it happened and what mercy and fairness need.
fact: Something lost still belongs to its owner, even if you are the one who found it.
fact: "You shall not steal" is one of the Ten Commandments (Exodus 20:15).
fact: Exodus is one of the five books of the Torah, so Jews and Christians share the Ten Commandments.
fact: If a found thing has a name or address, the right thing is to return it whole, with nothing taken out.
fact: Whether the owner is rich or poor does not change whose the thing is.
fact: If money is found with no name and no way to find the owner, it is best to hand it to an adult, a teacher or the police; what to do after that is disputed, and some people give it to charity.
fact: Keeping too much change that a shopkeeper gave by mistake is a way of taking what is not yours.
fact: Borrowing without asking takes away the owner's right to say no, even if you give the thing back.
fact: Copying a game, film or song without paying takes away the money its makers earned by their work.
fact: Copying someone's homework is unfair to the person who did the work and stops you from learning.
fact: If you think a friend has stolen something, you have a duty to act, starting by talking to them or a trusted adult.
fact: In the novel Les Misérables, Jean Valjean is sent to prison for years for stealing a loaf of bread to feed his sister's hungry children.
fact: Taking is wrong, but people disagree about how much need and mercy should change the punishment (disputed).
fact: The Bible teaches care for the poor, for example by letting the hungry gather leftover grain at the edges of fields (Leviticus 19:9-10).
exercise: Is "finders keepers" a fair rule? | No, a lost thing still belongs to its owner | Yes, once you find something it becomes yours by law | Yes, because the owner should have been more careful | No, but only if the thing costs a lot of money
exercise: Which commandment is about taking things that belong to others? | You shall not steal | Honour your father and mother | Remember the Sabbath day | You shall not murder
exercise: You find a ten-pound note in the playground. What is the best thing to do? | Hand it to a teacher; the owner may be looking for it | Keep it, because nobody saw you pick it up | Spend it on sweets for everyone, so it is shared fairly | Leave it lying there, because it is not your problem
exercise: You find a wallet with money and an address. What is the best-reasoned action? | Return it whole, imagining how worried the owner must feel | Take a little money as a reward, then post the rest back | Keep it, because people who lose wallets deserve to | Throw it away, because returning it is too much trouble
exercise: Would it matter if the wallet's owner were very rich? | No, it is still theirs, and fairness does not depend on wealth | Yes, rich people will not miss it, so you may keep it | Yes, you should only return things to people who are poor and really need them | No, because rich people always offer the biggest rewards
exercise: You find five dollars in the street with no name and nobody around. What do many people think is fairest? | Hand it in if you can; if no owner comes, some give it to charity | Keep it secretly, because money in the street belongs to anyone | Leave it there, because touching found money is always stealing | Give it to the first stranger you meet, so it is shared
exercise: A shopkeeper gives you too much change by mistake. What should you do? | Give it back, because keeping it is taking what is not yours | Keep it, because it was the shopkeeper's mistake, not yours | Keep it but buy something there next week to make it fair | Keep it only if the shop is a big chain that is rich and will never notice
exercise: Why is borrowing without asking a problem, even if you give it back? | It takes away the owner's right to say no | It is fine, as long as nothing gets broken | It is a problem only if you keep it for a week | It is a problem only if the owner is an adult
exercise: Why can copying a game or song without paying be wrong? | It takes the pay that its makers earned by their work | It is fine, because a copy does not take anything away | It is only wrong if the song is a very famous one | It is only wrong because the computer might break
exercise: Who is harmed when someone copies homework? | The one who did the work, and the copier, who does not learn | Nobody, because homework does not really matter at all | Only the teacher, who has to mark the very same work twice over | Only the copier, if they happen to get caught
exercise: Your friend shows off a new bike, and you think it might be stolen. What is the best-reasoned first step? | Ask your friend kindly, and tell a trusted adult if worried | Say nothing, because it is never your business what friends do | Tell the whole school your friend is a thief, so everyone knows | Ask to borrow it, because a stolen bike does not belong to anyone
exercise: Why can knowing about wrongdoing give you a duty to act? | Because someone is losing their property and you could help put it right | Because you will get a big reward if you report it to the police quickly | Because knowing about it means you did it yourself | It cannot; knowing something is never a reason to act
exercise: In Les Misérables, why was Jean Valjean sent to prison? | He stole a loaf of bread to feed hungry children | He found a wallet and kept the money inside | He copied his friend's homework at school | He borrowed a horse without asking the owner
exercise: Should someone who steals bread to feed their family be punished? What is the best-reasoned answer? | Taking is wrong, but need matters too, so mercy may be fairer | They should be punished as hard as possible, because rules are rules and that is all | They did nothing wrong at all, because hungry people may take anything they want | It does not matter, because shops have plenty of bread anyway
exercise: How did the Bible tell farmers to help hungry people? | Leave grain at the edges of fields for the poor to gather | Give all their land to the poor every single year | Sell bread to the poor at twice the normal price on market days | Lock their barns so nobody could ever take grain
exercise: What Christian belief makes people careful with things that are not theirs? | Everything is God's gift; treat others' things as you would want yours treated | Things do not matter at all, so it is fine to take anything | Only things in church belong to God, so others can be taken | People who lose things are being punished by God, so it is fine to keep whatever they lose
test: Your mate shouts, "Finders keepers, losers weepers, that phone's mine now!" How do you put him right? | It's still the owner's phone, they'll be gutted, hand it in | He's right, the law says anything you find is yours | Only keep it if it's an old phone nobody wants | Sell it quick before the owner turns up looking
test: Mum gets home and says, "The bloke at the till gave me a tenner too much, result!" What is the best-reasoned reply? | It's his mistake but still the shop's money | Brilliant, shops are rich so it doesn't matter | Keep it, because shopkeepers overcharge people anyway | Keep it, because you only steal when you take it on purpose
test: Your sister took your headphones without asking, then says, "Chill, I gave 'em back, didn't I?" What is the best answer? | You gave them back, but you should have asked; they're mine | You're right, borrowing without asking is fine if you return it | I'm telling everyone you're a thief and nobody should trust you | Fine, then I'll take your stuff without asking too
test: A classmate who is not religious asks, "Why would you give back a wallet if nobody would ever know?" What is the most thoughtful answer? | It's the owner's and they'd worry; and God sees and cares | Because Christians are scared of getting struck by lightning | Because anyone who keeps a wallet must go to prison for life | I wouldn't, I'd keep it if nobody would ever know
test: Your cousin says, "Downloading films for free off that dodgy site is fine, it's not like stealing a DVD." Is he right? | No, the makers lose the pay for their work | Yes, nothing is stolen if you never touch a real object | Yes, because film makers are all rich so they will not notice | No, but only because the computer might get a virus
test: Your friend whispers, "Gimme your maths sheet, I'll just change a few answers, Miss won't notice." What is the best-reasoned reply? | I'll help you understand it, but copying isn't fair | Sure, copying is fine as long as you change some answers | No way, you're too thick to do maths anyway | Only if you pay me two quid for each answer
test: Your brother says, "Jean Valjean stole bread so he was a bad guy, end of." What is a fairer view? | Stealing was wrong, but he did it to feed hungry children | He was a hero, so stealing is fine for anyone who is hungry | He was a bad guy because people in old books are always bad | It does not matter, because the story is made up anyway
test: You find a fiver on the pavement outside the busy shop. Nobody's around. Which is the best-reasoned choice? | Ask in the shop if anyone lost it, or hand it in, rather than just pocket it | Pocket it, because money on the pavement belongs to nobody | Tear it up so that nobody gets it and it stays fair | Run away from it, because touching lost money is a crime
test: Your friend's "new" bike still has someone else's name scratched on it, and he goes quiet when you ask. What is the best choice? | Talk to him, and tell a trusted adult if still worried | Say nothing and ask for a ride, because it's a cool bike | Shout "thief" at him in front of the whole class | Scratch your own name on it too, so it is shared
test: A judge at the olympiad asks: "If a rich man loses his wallet, isn't it fairer to give the money to the poor?" What is the best-reasoned answer? | No, it is still his money, not yours to give | Yes, taking from the rich to help the poor is always fair | Yes, because rich people do not deserve their money | No, because the poor would only waste it on silly things
test: A Jewish classmate says, "The commandment not to steal is in our Torah too." What is the best response? | Yes, the Ten Commandments are shared by Jews and Christians | No, the Ten Commandments belong only to Christians | Yes, but it means something totally different for Christians | No, the Ten Commandments were written by the Romans
test: Grandad says, "Leave the edges of your field for the hungry, that's in the Bible." What does this show? | The Bible teaches care for the poor alongside respect for property | The Bible says nobody should own any land at all | The Bible says farmers must give away all their crops | The Bible says hungry people must work for free
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the ownership of an item that has been lost? | Ownership remains vested in the original owner notwithstanding its discovery by another | Ownership passes automatically to whosoever first discovers the item | Ownership is extinguished entirely upon the item being mislaid | Ownership passes to the state immediately upon loss
exam: Given that excess change has been tendered in error by a shopkeeper, identify the course of action most consistent with the commandment "You shall not steal". | Restitution of the excess sum to the shopkeeper without delay | Retention of the excess sum, the error being solely the shopkeeper's | Retention of the excess sum pending a further purchase | Donation of the excess sum to a third party of the finder's choosing
exam: Identify the principal reason for which borrowing without permission is regarded as morally deficient, notwithstanding the return of the item. | It deprives the owner of the right to grant or refuse consent | It invariably results in damage to the item concerned | It is permissible solely where the owner is a minor | It constitutes no wrong whatsoever provided the item is returned
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning Jean Valjean in the novel Les Misérables. | He was imprisoned for the theft of a loaf of bread intended to feed hungry children | He was imprisoned for the retention of a wallet he had discovered lying in the street | He was rewarded by the authorities for the theft of a loaf of bread | He was a shopkeeper who tendered excess change to his customers
